The Copilot Runtime, introduced at Build 2024, already showed how local AI models can power low-latency, context-aware features directly on device. This year, the company is poised to detail a full agent framework that lets developers build, deploy, and monetize agents across Windows, Edge, and Office.\nThese agents won't be simple chatbots. They'll tap into the Windows semantic index — a vectorized representation of everything on your PC — to perform tasks like summarizing a messy OneDrive folder, auto-filling expense reports based on screenshots, or dynamically rescheduling meetings when new emails arrive. Privacy will be front and center; Microsoft must convince users that on-device processing and encrypted vectors keep sensitive data off its servers.\nThe Copilot Platform Gets a Developer Edition\nA major pillar of Build 2026 will be the Copilot platform itself. Leaked internal roadmaps suggest Microsoft wants Copilot to become the new app model — where \\\"extensions\\\" replace traditional plugins and integrate with Windows at the system level. Developers will gain full access to a Copilot SDK, enabling third-party actions that live inside the Copilot side panel, File Explorer, or even the taskbar.\nExpect deep integrations with GitHub. The GitHub Copilot extension for Visual Studio Code already hits over 1.5 million developers; now Microsoft is closing the loop between writing code and running it on Windows. New APIs will let developers publish copilot actions directly from their CI/CD pipelines, with automatic packaging for Windows Copilot Runtime. This blurs the line between development and deployment: you debug an agent in VS Code, test it in a sandboxed Windows container, and ship it to the Microsoft Store with one click.\nAccountability tools will also debut. Microsoft is under pressure to prevent Copilot from hallucinating actions that could corrupt files or break workflows. A \\\"Copilot Guard\\\" feature, referenced in recent Windows Insider builds, will likely be finalized at Build. It sandboxes each agent action and prompts for user confirmation before touching system settings or critical data.\nWindows on Arm: The Snapdragon X Era\nWindows on Arm finally hit its stride with Qualcomm's Snapdragon X Elite and X Plus chips, and Build 2026 will spotlight the platform's maturation. Microsoft's own Surface Pro 10 and Surface Laptop 6, both Arm-based, have sold solidly, but developer tools lag. That changes here.\nA new Arm-native Visual Studio 2025 version (build 19.x) is expected to enter general availability around Build, bringing full C++ and .NET compilation without x86 emulation. The Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) gains Arm-optimized distros and GPU passthrough for local AI training, addressing a long-standing complaint from data scientists. Microsoft will also detail a high-performance emulation layer — internally called \\\"Prism 2\\\" — that runs x86-64 apps on Arm at near-native speeds, closing the gap for legacy line-of-business software.\nHardware partners will be on stage too. Dell, Lenovo, and ASUS are preparing a wave of Arm-based business laptops with NPUs that exceed 45 TOPS, necessary for Windows AI features like Recall and Studio Effects. A new Snapdragon X Plus Gen 2 chip, built on a 3nm process, may be teased for a late-2026 release, promising 20% better single-core performance and even longer battery life.\nAI-Powered Developer Tooling Goes Mainstream\nBuild has always been a developer conference, and the 2026 edition doubles down on AI-assisted coding. GitHub Copilot X — the next iteration powered by a fine-tuned GPT-5-class model — will move from chat to full project awareness. Imagine telling Copilot \\\"Add dark mode to this WinUI 3 app\\\" and watching it modify XAML, styles, and resource dictionaries across dozens of files, then create a pull request with automated tests.\nThe new \\\"Copilot Workspace\\\" feature, first shown in tech previews, gets its public debut. It lets developers plan, code, and review...\nFirst, expect Windows Copilot to become vastly more competent by late 2026. Instead of simple web searches, Copilot will act like a personal system administrator: it will troubleshoot driver issues, free up disk space intelligently, and even configure complex multi-monitor setups for you — all by parsing natural language commands.\nFor creative professionals, agents will finally bridge the gap between local apps and cloud AI. Adobe and Microsoft are deepening their pact; look for Photoshop and Premiere Pro plugins that tap Copilot agents to find licensed stock assets, apply batch edits across projects, or render previews on Azure GPUs — all from within the Windows file system. File Explorer itself gets an agent sidebar that can summarize selected documents, translate text in images, or convert a folder of drafts into a formatted OneNote notebook.\nThe Hybrid Conference Experience\nBuild 2026 won't be just about products — it's about defining the AI-first PC. Microsoft believes the desktop is the ultimate edge device, capable of running 10-billion-parameter models locally while staying connected to cloud reasoning engines. The conference will outline a two-year roadmap where Windows evolves from a passive operating system into an active participant in your workflow, with agents acting as intermediaries between apps, data, and intentions.\nThere are risks. Competitors like Google, with its ChromeOS and Gemini integration, and Apple, which is baking on-device AI into macOS 17, are racing to similar endpoints. Developers may grow weary of constant AI pivots if the tooling feels unreliable. And enterprise adoption hinges on security proofs that Microsoft has struggled to deliver in the past. But if the demos at Build 2026 deliver on their promise, the Windows ecosystem could finally get the AI jolt it has needed since the Copilot launch two years ago.\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：微软Build大会 2026年6月3日 AI PC\n\n网页标题：Build 2026: Microsoft’s Windows AI Models, Copilot Super App, and Dev Setup Reset\n\n网页链接：https://windowsnews.ai/article/build-2026-microsofts-windows-ai-models-copilot-super-app-and-dev-setup-reset.421337\n\n发布时间：2026-06-02T06:47:12+08:00\n\n网页内容：Build 2026: Microsoft’s Windows AI Models, Copilot Super App, and Dev Setup Reset\nWindows News Team\n16 minutes ago\nMicrosoft Build 2026 will take place June 2–3 in San Francisco, focusing on new Windows AI models for on-device processing, a Microsoft-built AI reasoning model, a Copilot super app with a plugin marketplace, and a one-click developer machine setup tool. The event signals a major shift toward AI-native Windows development.\nA pivotal moment for the AI eraMicrosoft has officially confirmed that Build 2026 will take place June 2–3 at the Fort Mason Center in San Francisco, with a hybrid format that brings developers together in person and via livestream. The conference, long the company’s flagship event for the developer community, will this year zero in on a raft of new artificial intelligence capabilities woven deep into the Windows platform. According to early agenda leaks, the keynotes and technical sessions will spotlight on-device Windows AI models, a new Microsoft-built AI reasoning model, a dramatically expanded Copilot “super app” strategy, and a long-awaited overhaul of the developer setup experience on Windows.\nRegistrations are expected to open in late April, and demand is already surging. Build 2026 arrives at a moment when AI is no longer a novelty on the Windows desktop but a foundational layer that Microsoft wants every developer to target. The choice of Fort Mason, a venue smaller than previous Build locales like the Washington State Convention Center, signals a more focused, hands-on event with deep-dive workshops and limited seating for in-person attendees.\nWindows AI Models: on-device intelligence takes center stage\nThe biggest headline for Windows developers is the imminent rollout of dedicated Windows AI models that run locally on the NPU (Neural Processing Unit) inside modern Copilot+ PCs. Microsoft first hinted at this vision with the Windows Copilot Runtime last year, but at Build 2026 the company will make good on its promise by opening these models to third-party applications. Sources close to the planning describe a new set of APIs under the Windows AI Studio umbrella that let any Win32, UWP, or WinUI 3 app tap into on-device language understanding, image generation, and real-time audio transcription without sending data to the cloud.\n“This is the moment where Windows becomes an AI operating system at the silicon level,” said one Microsoft program manager who requested anonymity because they were not authorized to speak publicly. “Developers will be able to add ChatGPT-style reasoning and creative features to their apps with as little as a few lines of code, and everything runs offline. That’s a game changer for privacy, latency, and cost.”\nThe models themselves span a range of sizes optimized for the Qualcomm Snapdragon X, Intel Lunar Lake, and AMD Ryzen AI 300 series NPUs. The smallest model, code-named Hermes-1B, handles text classification, summarization, and sentiment analysis. A mid-tier model, Atlas-3B, powers contextual app actions—imagine a photo editor that suggests edits based on natural language commands. At the top sits Titan-7B, a larger model capable of generating images, composing emails, and debugging code directly within Visual Studio Code, all without an internet connection.\nCrucially, Microsoft will also offer a model customization pipeline. Using synthetic data generation and low-rank adaptation (LoRA), developers can fine-tune these base models on their own datasets right inside Azure Machine Learning or a local WSL instance, then package the fine-tuned adapter alongside their app in the Microsoft Store. This could spark a wave of specialized AI assistants for niche professional workflows—legal contract analysis, architectural drafting, scientific notation parsing—that previously required expensive cloud GPU cycles.\nMicrosoft’s own AI reasoning model\nWhile on-device models handle routine tasks, Microsoft plans to unveil an in-house foundation model focused on multi-step reasoning. Dubbed “Project Athena” in internal documents, this model is designed to compete directly with OpenAI’s o1 and Google DeepMind’s Gemini reasoning systems. Unlike the LLMs that currently power Copilot Chat, Project Athena breaks complex queries into discrete reasoning chains, validates each step against external knowledge bases, and can even challenge its own outputs when inconsistencies are detected.\nSatya Nadella is expected to demonstrate Athena solving a messy, real-world coding problem during the opening keynote: taking a sprawling legacy Visual Basic 6 codebase, analyzing dependencies, and refactoring it into a modern .NET 9 solution with proper error handling and test coverage—in under a minute. That demo, if successful, would electrify the developer audience. Microsoft envisions Athena as the thinking engine behind next-generation Copilot experiences, capable of understanding entire code repositories, build pipelines, and deployment scripts holistically rather than responding to isolated prompts.\nThe model will initially be available via Azure AI Studio and GitHub Models, with pay-as-you-go pricing based on reasoning tokens consumed. For enterprise developers, Microsoft will bundle Athena access with GitHub Copilot Enterprise subscriptions, giving organizations a powerful tool for architecture reviews, migration planning, and incident post-mortems. Privacy-conscious developers will also get a containerized version that runs inside Azure Confidential Computing enclaves, ensuring even Microsoft cannot see the code being analyzed.\nCopilot becomes a super app—and a platform\nPerhaps the most ambitious pillar of Build 2026 is the evolution of Copilot from a sidebar assistant into a full-blown super app and extension platform. The current Copilot app, while useful for quick questions and document summarization, has remained somewhat siloed. The upcoming release, code-named “Copilot Canvas,” tears down those silos by integrating a universal inbox, a project workspace, and a third-party plugin marketplace directly into the Windows shell.\nLeaked design mockups show a revamped Copilot interface that can snap to the side of the screen like the macOS Notification Center or float as a resizable window. At its heart is the “Canvas,” a persistent space where users can drop files, images, code snippets, and live web widgets. Copilot then maintains context across everything in the Canvas, allowing cross-application workflows that span Outlook, Word, Visual Studio, Power BI, and third-party apps like Figma, Stripe, and Monday.com—all without the user switching tools.\nThe plugin marketplace, expected to launch with around 200 vetted extensions, opens Copilot to the same kind of ecosystem that made ChatGPT a hit. Developers will be able to build and monetize plugins using a new Copilot SDK that supports TypeScript, Python, and C#. Plugins can tap into the Windows AI models for local inference, use Athena for reasoning, or call out to any REST API. Revenue sharing details are still under wraps, but Microsoft is leaning toward a 70/30 split in the developer’s favor after payment processing fees.\nFor Windows app developers, the super app represents a distribution channel that sidesteps the Microsoft Store. A Copilot plugin can surface right when a user asks a relevant question—for example, a CRM plugin could display a live customer record when the user asks “What’s the status of the...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：微软Build大会 2026年6月3日 AI PC\n\n网页标题：Build 2026 in San Francisco: Windows Becomes an AI Platform, Not Just an OS\n\n网页链接：https://windowsnews.ai/article/build-2026-in-san-francisco-windows-becomes-an-ai-platform-not-just-an-os.421296\n\n发布时间：2026-06-02T03:27:21+08:00\n\n网页内容：Build 2026 in San Francisco: Windows Becomes an AI Platform, Not Just an OS\nWindows News Team\n34 minutes ago\nMicrosoft Build 2026 takes place June 2-3 at Fort Mason Center in San Francisco, with a focus on transforming Windows into an AI platform. Expect major updates to Copilot, Windows AI runtime, Azure AI tools, and Windows on Arm, alongside on-device AI capabilities powered by NPUs.\ncopilot+ pcs microsoft build windows ai windows on arm ai developers azure ai\nTable of Contents\nWindows as the AI Canvas\nCopilot Everywhere\nAzure AI and Developer Tools\nMicrosoft Build 2026 will take place at Fort Mason Center in San Francisco from June 2–3, shifting the developer conference from its recent Seattle home to the heart of the AI startup scene. The venue choice alone telegraphs the company’s priorities: AI integration will dominate the agenda, with Windows taking center stage as an AI-first platform rather than a traditional desktop operating system.\nRegistration for the in-person event opened this week, with early-bird pricing at $1,525. A digital experience will also be available free of charge, continuing Microsoft's hybrid approach. But the real news is what Redmond plans to unveil: a new generation of AI-powered tools that blur the line between cloud and client, developer and machine, PC and intelligent agent.\nWindows as the AI Canvas\nMultiple sources close to the company indicate that Build 2026 will deliver the biggest Windows AI update since the Copilot+ PC launch in 2024. The operating system is set to become a runtime for AI agents, with new APIs that let developers embed AI capabilities directly into applications—bypassing the cloud and running on-device neural processing units (NPUs).\n\\\"Windows isn’t just a shell for launching apps anymore,\\\" said one Microsoft program manager who requested anonymity because they were not authorized to speak publicly. \\\"It’s the surface area for AI experiences. At Build, we’ll show how every Windows PC becomes an AI PC.\\\"\nThis shift was previewed at Ignite 2025, where Microsoft introduced Windows Copilot Runtime, a local inference engine that powers features like Recall, Click to Do, and live captioning. Build 2026 will flesh out the developer story: new Studio Effects for video, a local vector database for semantic search, and a dedicated AI toolkit in Visual Studio.\nExpect demonstrations of Windows PCs running large language models (LLMs) with 7 billion parameters entirely on-device, delivering sub-200ms response times for coding, document summarization, and real‑time translation without an internet connection.\nCopilot Everywhere\nCopilot will saturate every layer of the Microsoft stack. The company is likely to announce:\nNew model catalog entries, including the latest Small Language Models (SLMs) optimized for edge devices.\nEnhanced vector database integration with Azure Cosmos DB.\nA revamped AI Foundry portal that simplifies model evaluation and fine‑tuning.\nNVIDIA Blackwell GPU instances in preview, targeting enterprise AI workloads.\nVisual Studio and VS Code will both receive AI‑assisted development features. GitHub Copilot Workspace, currently in limited beta, may become generally available, allowing developers to describe a feature in plain English and get a complete pull request.\nWindows on Arm and the NPU Advantage\nWindows on Arm will have a major presence. With Qualcomm’s Snapdragon X Elite and now the X Plus Gen 2 chips driving a new wave of Copilot+ PCs, Microsoft needs to convince developers that Arm64 is a first‑class target. Build 2026 will offer hands‑on labs showing how to migrate x64 apps to Arm with minimal friction using the Arm64EC (Emulation Compatible) ABI.\nA dedicated “App Assure” session will address enterprise compatibility concerns. Microsoft is also expected to unveil a new Dev Kit for Arm64 with a powerful NPU, priced aggressively to attract indie developers.\nThe NPU itself becomes a platform differentiator. Microsoft will introduce an NPU‑aware task scheduler that lets Windows intelligently route AI workloads between CPU, GPU, and NPU based on power and performance profiles. This hybrid compute model is the foundation for always‑on AI features that don’t drain battery life.\nWhat to Expect from the Keynote\nCEO Satya Nadella’s opening keynote on June 2 will set the tone. Historically, Build keynotes have been used to announce major product milestones. This year, insiders suggest the keynote will position Microsoft’s AI as an open alternative to closed systems, with announcements around:\nA new commitment to open‑weight model availability on Azure.\nPartnerships with Meta for Llama 4 and Mistral for its next‑generation model.\nMulti‑agent frameworks that let AI agents from different vendors collaborate within a Windows‑hosted sandbox.\nThere is also...\nThe digital experience is free and includes live streams of all keynotes, on‑demand sessions, and a virtual networking platform.\nFort Mason Center offers a more intimate, campus‑like setting compared to the sprawling Washington State Convention Center. Microsoft will use multiple pavilions for breakout sessions, sponsor expos, and the popular \\\"Developer Zone\\\" where attendees can get one‑on‑one help from engineers.\nHotels in the Marina District are already filling up. Microsoft has negotiated a conference rate at several nearby properties, but attendees should book quickly.\nAnalysis: An OS That Thinks\nBuild 2026 arrives at an inflection point for personal computing. Copilot+ PCs have sold modestly—around 3.2 million units in Q1 2026 according to Canalys—but the long‑term vision is undeniable. Windows is being rebuilt around AI, not just as an add‑on but as a fundamental design principle.\nThis poses challenges. Privacy advocates will scrutinize local AI features that index user activity. Developers must learn new toolchains and design patterns. And enterprises need clear ROI to justify hardware refreshes with NPU‑equipped PCs.\nMicrosoft’s answer appears to be a “hybrid AI” approach: sensitive data stays on‑device, while complex queries leverage the cloud. The Build agenda will attempt to demonstrate that this architecture is mature, secure, and ready for prime time.\nFor Windows watchers, the most exciting sessions may be the deep dives into the Windows AI subsystem. Rumored additions include a local RAG (Retrieval‑Augmented Generation) pipeline that lets any Windows app index its own content and expose it to a local LLM. If delivered, this could finally make the decades‑old dream of a truly personal AI assistant—one that knows your files, emails, and calendar—a reality.\nMicrosoft Build 2026 runs June 2–3 at Fort Mason Center in San Francisco. The opening keynote begins at 9:00 AM Pacific on June 2. Whether you’re in the Bay Area or joining online, this year’s conference will make one thing clear: Windows is no longer just an operating system. It’s an AI platform.\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：微软Build大会 2026年6月3日 AI PC\n\n网页标题：Microsoft will unveil new AI models and Windows upgrades at the Build Developer Conference\n\n网页链接：https://www.c114pro.com/ainews/168598.html\n\n发布时间：2026-06-01T23:13:53+08:00\n\n网页内容：Microsoft will unveil new AI models and Windows upgrades at the Build Developer Conference\n1 hour ago / Read about 0 minute\nAuthor：小编\nMicrosoft will host the Build Developer Conference from June 2 to 3, 2026, at Fort Mason in San Francisco, with attendance scaled down to approximately 2,500 participants and a more compact venue to enhance interactive experiences. This conference represents a critical opportunity for Microsoft to regain developer trust, showcase AI technological achievements, and outline future product roadmaps, particularly in response to declining trust in Windows and GitHub. Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella and other executives, along with several industry experts, will attend, focusing on core topics such as the deployment of AI Agent technology, deep integration of GitHub and Azure, and Windows as an AI inference platform. The conference will also release 50 free tools covering areas like code editing, development environment configuration, and AI model testing, helping developers familiarize themselves with the technological ecosystem in advance.\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：微软Build大会 2026年6月3日 AI PC\n\n网页标题：Microsoft Build 2026 Live Stream June 2: AI PC Reset, Copilot, and Windows on Arm\n\n网页链接：https://windowsforum.com/threads/microsoft-build-2026-live-stream-june-2-ai-pc-reset-copilot-and-windows-on-arm.421334/\n\n发布时间：2026-06-02T06:14:09+08:00\n\n网页内容：Microsoft Build 2026 Live Stream June 2: AI PC Reset, Copilot, and Windows on Arm\n12 minutes ago\nTags\nai pc\ncopilot microsoft build windows on arm\n12 minutes ago\nMicrosoft Build 2026 begins Tuesday, June 2, 2026, at 12:30 p.m. Eastern / 9:30 a.m. Pacific with a Satya Nadella keynote streamed online, while Microsoft runs the two-day developer conference from San Francisco and online through June 3. The simple answer is that viewers can watch through Microsoft’s Build site and YouTube, with live coverage from major tech outlets. The more interesting answer is that this year’s Build is less a software conference than a public reset of Microsoft’s AI-era Windows strategy. After a year of Copilot saturation, enterprise pushback, and fresh Arm PC intrigue, Build 2026 is where Microsoft has to prove that “AI PC” means more than another button on the taskbar.Build Returns as Microsoft’s AI Pitch Gets Harder to Sell\nBuild has always been Microsoft’s developer state-of-the-union address, but the 2026 edition arrives with unusual tension. The company is still plainly committed to an AI-first future, yet the reception to that future has become more complicated than the launch decks suggested. Developers want capabilities, admins want control, and everyday Windows users want to know whether the operating system is being improved or merely decorated.\nThat is why the “how to watch” details matter less than the timing. The keynote lands at the intersection of three Microsoft stories: the next phase of Copilot, the future of Windows on Arm, and the company’s attempt to persuade developers that AI agents are now a platform rather than a demo category. If Build is supposed to be Microsoft’s most optimistic event, Build 2026 is optimism under cross-examination.\nMicrosoft’s framing is still familiar. The company wants developers to build for what it calls the era of AI, and it wants enterprises to see Copilot and agent tooling as the next abstraction layer above apps, files, meetings, and data. But the old Build formula—announce APIs, show a slick demo, promise productivity—has to work harder when the audience has spent the past year dealing with confusing licensing, changing Copilot placements, and Windows features that sometimes feel like marketing surfaces in search of workflows.\nThat gives this year’s keynote a sharper edge. Nadella does not merely need to announce new tools; he needs to make Microsoft’s AI strategy feel coherent again.\nThe Livestream Is the Easy Part\nFor viewers, the mechanics are straightforward. Microsoft Build 2026 runs June 2 and June 3, with the opening keynote scheduled for midday Eastern time and mid-morning Pacific time. Microsoft is offering the event online, and the keynote is expected to be available via livestream, including YouTube distribution and Microsoft’s own event pages.\nThat puts the keynote squarely in the workday for U.S. viewers, early evening for much of Europe, and overnight or early morning for parts of Asia-Pacific. The timing also overlaps a busy hardware week, with Computex activity in Taipei feeding speculation about new silicon, new PC reference designs, and new developer targets. In other words, this is not just a Microsoft show; it is a Microsoft show surrounded by PC industry signaling.\nEngadget and other tech publications are expected to liveblog the keynote, which is useful because Build keynotes can be dense in the way only developer conferences can be. A single segment may jump from Azure infrastructure to GitHub tooling to Windows APIs to Copilot extensibility in the space of a few minutes. A liveblog is often the better way to track what actually shipped, what was previewed, and what was merely positioned as inevitable.\nMicrosoft’s own blog will almost certainly be the official record for announcements. That matters because Build demos routinely blur the line between “available today,” “private preview,” “public preview,” and “coming later this year.” Anyone planning around announcements should wait for the official posts and docs before assuming that a keynote feature is deployable in production.\nThe Windows 11 Backlash Has Changed the Room\nWindows 11 has not failed, but it has become a more contested product than Microsoft would like. Hardware requirements, UI changes, account nudges, ads, defaults, telemetry concerns, and Copilot integration have all contributed to a sense among power users that Windows is increasingly being managed for Microsoft’s strategic goals rather than the user’s immediate convenience.\nThat sentiment matters at Build because developers are often Windows’ most influential critics. They may not represent the average PC user, but they shape the software ecosystem, advise organizations, and influence procurement conversations. When developers complain that Windows is noisier, more intrusive, or less predictable, those complaints travel.\nMicrosoft appears to understand some of this. Recent reporting and product changes point to a selective retreat from the most gratuitous Copilot placements, including the removal or renaming of certain AI-branded entry points in built-in apps. Enterprise admins have also gained more control over removing the Copilot app on managed devices under certain conditions.\nThat does not mean Microsoft is abandoning AI in Windows. Far from it. The company’s direction appears to be toward deeper integration in fewer, more defensible places: search, task switching, app launching, Microsoft 365 context, and developer workflows. If Microsoft can make Copilot feel like a capability that appears when it is useful rather than a mascot that follows users around the OS, the backlash may soften.\nBut that is a product discipline problem, not a keynote problem. Build can announce the new direction. Windows users will judge it later, one update at a time.\nThe AI PC Needs Developers More Than It Needs Stickers\nThe phrase “AI PC” has spent the last couple of years doing too much work. It can mean a laptop with a neural processing unit. It can mean a device certified for certain Copilot+ features. It can mean a premium Windows machine with local inference capabilities. It can also mean, less charitably, a normal PC sold during an AI upgrade cycle.\nBuild 2026 is where Microsoft has to make the category more concrete for developers. Hardware only matters if software uses it. Local AI only matters if applications can rely on it. NPUs only matter if developers understand what workloads should run locally, which APIs are stable, and how performance, privacy, battery life, and fallback behavior are supposed to work across a fragmented PC market.\nThat is harder than it sounds. Windows developers already live in a world of different CPU architectures, GPU vendors, driver stacks, enterprise policies, store rules, and deployment models. Adding local AI acceleration can either become a powerful new baseline or another matrix of “works on this device, not on that one.”\nMicrosoft’s incentive is to simplify the story. It wants developers to think of Windows as the best client platform for AI applications, not merely because Windows has market share, but because it can orchestrate cloud models, local models, enterprise data, identity, security policy, and specialized...\n\n\n--------\n搜索关键词：微软Build大会 2026年6月3日 AI PC\n\n网页标题：群联电子与英特尔合作推出AI PC内存扩充技术\n\n网页链接：https://www.coze.cn/share-article/201780367295584352\n\n发布时间：2026-06-02T10: